<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nauru</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Naoero, Naoero, Pleasant Island</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nauru, also known as Naoero, is an island country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micronesia">Micronesia</a> in the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, and one of the smallest states in the world. Micronesians first settled it at least 3,000 years ago, and it had twelve traditional clans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> annexed it in 1888. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> occupied it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, after which it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied it from 1942. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory after the war and became independent on 31 January 1968. Phosphate-rock mining, begun in 1900, stripped most of its interior.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nauru is an oval island of 21 square kilometres in the south-western Pacific, with its nearest neighbour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaba">Banaba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a>, about 300 kilometres to the east. A fringing coral reef surrounds it and has prevented the building of a seaport, although channels let small boats reach the shore. A fertile coastal strip lies inland from the beach, and coral cliffs surround a central plateau whose highest point, Command Ridge, is 71 metres above sea level.</p><p>There are no streams, so water is gathered from roofs or was brought as ballast on ships returning for phosphate. The name in Nauruan is Naoero; the ethnologist Paul Hambruch derived it from a phrase meaning I go to the beach, but the linguist Alois Kayser called that a folk etymology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little is known of Nauruan prehistory, although a long isolation accounts for the distinct language. The twelve traditional clans are represented in the twelve-pointed star on the flag, and descent was traced through the mother. Inhabitants caught juvenile milkfish, accustomed them to freshwater and raised them in Buada Lagoon. When a German protectorate was proclaimed in 1888, Nauru became the official name; the island had previously been known in English as Pleasant Island.</p><p>The economy was strongest in the 1970s, with output peaking in 1981, and mining declined from the early 1980s. The Nauru Phosphate Royalties Trust was meant to support the citizens after the reserves ran out, but mismanagement reduced its assets considerably. Failed investments included financing Leonardo the Musical in 1993, and the Mercure Hotel in Sydney and Nauru House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> were sold in 2004 to finance debts.</p><p>In 2006 about 1,500 people left in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriation</a> of immigrant workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuvalu">Tuvalu</a> after layoffs in the mines. Angam Day, on 26 October, celebrates the recovery of the Nauruan population after the two world wars and the 1920 influenza epidemic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yaren is the de facto capital. Nauruan and English are the official languages, and the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 12,511 residents. The country is a parliamentary republic whose 19 members of parliament elect the president, and it uses the Australian dollar. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1999 and has no armed forces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> being responsible for its defence under an informal agreement. In May 2026 a constitutional amendment changed the official English name to Republic of Naoero.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nauru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
